window.docContents[52].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#proof-irrelevance":{"contents":"When we view the proposition being proved by even_plus4 as a\nfunction type, one interesting point becomes apparent: The second\nargument's type, Even n, mentions the value of the first\nargument, n.\n\nWhile such dependent types are not found in most mainstream\nprogramming languages, they can be quite useful in programming\ntoo, as the flurry of activity in the functional programming\ncommunity over the past couple of decades\n(and recently, most notably Lean!) demonstrates.\nDependent types sit at the \"highest corner\" of the Lambda Cube\n(see the Stlc chapter of Type Systems): types can depend on values\nin addition to other types.\n\n\n\nNotice that both implication (→) and quantification (∀)\ncorrespond to functions on evidence.  In fact, they are really the\nsame thing: → is just a shorthand for a degenerate use of\n∀ where there is no dependency, i.e., no need to give a\nname to the type on the left-hand side of the arrow:\n\n   ∀ (x : Nat), Nat\n=  ∀ (_ : Nat), Nat\n=  Nat        → Nat\n\n\n\n\nRecall the definition of Even:inductive Even : Nat → Prop where\n  | zero : Even 0\n  | succ_succ (n : Nat) (h : Even n) : Even (n + 2)\nWhat is the type of this expression?fun (n : Nat) =>\n  fun (h : Even n) =>\n     Even.succ_succ (n + 2) (Even.succ_succ n h)\n(A) ∀ (n : Nat), Even n(B) ∀ (n : Nat), Even (n + 2)(C) ∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even n(D) ∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even (n + 2)(E) ∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even (n + 4)(F) Not typeable(E)#check (fun (n : Nat) =>\n          fun (h : Even n) =>\n            Even.succ_succ (n + 2) (Even.succ_succ n h)\n        : ∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even (n + 4))\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence\u0009Quantifiers, Implications, Functions","header":"10.2.1. Dependency, Equivalence, and Proof Irrelevance","id":"/ProofObjects/#proof-irrelevance"}});